2026-09-13. Root's static review found three concrete preparation gaps. No candidate test has run.

Inbound JSON now receives the same passive-tree limits as outbound data. This rejects exponent overflow to infinity, oversized integers, strings, container counts and aggregate structure after the bounded JSON parse. The parser still receives at most 262,144 bytes and depth 10; this does not claim a preallocation node-count limit inside json.loads. Authenticated negative cases will exercise these refusals.

Rename memory_budget to payload_budget. It bounds the aggregate approved file bytes, not peak resident memory. This implementation can hold completed file buffers plus two representations of the current file while joining its chunks; a conservative payload-only transient bound is twice the payload budget plus one read buffer. Container/object overhead, parser/native allocations and interpreter memory are additional and not capped here. A real full-buffer route needs measured peak memory and a separately reviewed process memory policy before acceptance; no advertised model is silently dropped.

Factory build now refuses before construction unless the supplied fixed owned-runtime module's _RUNTIME is the exact registry created by this bootstrap. It rechecks the binding before publication and release. This proposal requires the trusted fixed worker caller to install that binding; it does not mutate an arbitrary supplied module or pretend the native bootstrap is already admitted. Tests cover absent, foreign and changed bindings. Registry revocation remains independent of factory serialization; it does not stop native work already entered.

Channel encode/decode now hold one lock through sequence, MAC and result construction and refuse reentrant operations. Revocation takes the same lock. An operation committed before a concurrent revoke can return its passive bytes afterward; operations entering after revocation refuse. No frame or object record supplies native-process quiescence.
